Content-Type: text/plain; charset=US-ASCII; name="BDY.TXT"
Content-Disposition: inline; filename="BDY.TXT"

Bryan Lavell and DB Wolf helped me with this one. The solution is to 
add an entry as shown below.

Thanks.

Use lprsetup and create a remote printer entry for the NT machine.  You 
will
need to have a name with no white space in it on the NT machine.  
Example:
HPprinter as opposed to HP Printer.  Here is my printcap entry for my NT
print server.  Note that I don't do any thing fancy, just straight 
printing.

lp|lp0|0|lp0 (test it):\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lperr:\
        :lp=:\
        :rm=ntgate:\ # My NT machine
        :rp=HP:\  # My NT print que name
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd:

Bryan


--openmail-part-04ca0b02-00000001--

