From: Jeff_Young () paribas ! com
Date: Fri, 02 Jul 1999 02:54:58 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Amnesiac boot
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079058315672



Many thanks to



gunther.feuereisen@vodafone.com.au

pjahnz@gfs.com



Who both provided solutions to my problem, which is now fixed.  The problem was



     | When we do a full boot from the console i.e boot_osflags=a
     | the system boots into
     | the following state
     |
     | Login banner.  Instead of the usual we get
     |
     |           Digital UNIX 4.0 (Amnesiac) console
     |           login:
     |
     | No addition swap space is added ( only default available)
     | No licences are loaded
     | Can't init3 the machine.
     | None of the startup scripts are run. i.e /etc/rc*d



The solutions are

gunther.feuereisen@vodafone.com.au

     I've had this before; if you get corruption in root_domain using AdvFS,
     you get this sort of corruption; nothing loads, hostanme reverts to
     Amnesiac
     and a whole swag of problems.

     If you boot in single user and do a verify -r root_domain you should see a
     whole
     bunch of errors.

     Best fix: get a backup of root_domain, boot off CD; blow away root_domain
     (completely
     delete the filedomain; just removing the fileset isn't enough), recreate
the
     domain
     and fileset again and restore.

pjahnz@gfs.com ( This was the actual problem )

      It was due to files being located beneath my mounted filesystem.  Once I
     unmounted everything, cd'd to the mount point, removed the files, and
     booted back up, we were good to go.



It seems that the machine has been patched without the /var filesystem mounted.
     This process created a /var directory containing files and directories
     which was actually in the root filesystem and beneath the /var mount point.
     These were moved and the machine booted as it should.



Thanks again



Jeff




